a.eu】\nL45:   * 【37†Slovenia†results.elections.europa.eu】\nL46:   * 【38†Spain†results.elections.europa.eu】\nL47:   * 【39†BG - български】\nL48:   * 【40†ES - español】\nL49:   * 【41†CS - čeština】\nL50:   * 【42†DA - dansk】\nL51:   * 【43†DE - Deutsch】\nL52:   * 【44†ET - eesti keel】\nL53:   * 【45†EL - ελληνικά】\nL54:   * 【46†EN - English】\nL55:   * 【47†FR - français】\nL56:   * 【48†GA - Gaeilge】\nL57:   * 【49†HR - hrvatski】\nL58:   * 【50†IT - italiano】\nL59:   * 【51†LV - latviešu valoda】\nL60:   * 【52†LT - lietuvių kalba】\nL61:   * 【53†HU - magyar】\nL62:   * 【54†MT - Malti】\nL63:   * 【55†NL - Nederlands】\nL64:   * 【56†PL - polski】\nL65:   * 【57†PT - português】\nL66:   * 【58†RO - română】\nL67:   * 【59†SK - slovenčina】\nL68:   * 【60†SL - slovenščina】\nL69:   * 【61†FI - suomi】\nL70:   * 【62†SV - svenska】\nL71: Information on this website relates to the 2024 European elections. It will be updated in time for the next elections which will take place in 2029.\nL72: \nL73: European elections 6-9 June 2024\nL74: # How European elections work\nL75: \nL76: Every five years, European Union citizens elect Members of the European Parliament.\nL77: \nL78:   * ## What are European elections? How are MEPs elected?\nL79: \nL80: In European elections, citizens of European Union countries elect their representatives as Members of the European Parliament (MEPs).\nL81: \nL82: Voting in European elections\nL83: \nL84: Transcript - Voting in European elections\nL85: \nL86: Why are European elections important?\nL87: \nL88: The European elections are one of the biggest democratic votes in the world!\nL89: Every 5 years, voters choose over 700 Members of the European Parliament who represent you!\nL90: \nL91: Once elected, they sit in political groups based on shared beliefs.\nL92: \nL93: EU citizens can vote and stand for election in their own country, or the EU country they live in.\nL94: \nL95: Your vote decides how Parliament decides on the things that matter:\nL96: \nL97: the economy, energy and climate, Europe’s place in the world.\nL98: \nL99: By voting you make your voice heard, you help choose the next European Parliament and you decide on your future!\nL100: Democracy in action\nL101: \nL102: European Parliament\nL103: \nL104: Share\nL105: \nL106:     * 【63†Share on Facebook - What are European elections? How are MEPs elected?†www.facebook.com】\nL107:     * 【64†Share on Twiter - What are European elections? How are MEPs elected?†twitter.com】\nL108:     * 【65†Share on LinkedIn - What are European elections? How are MEPs elected?†www.linkedin.com】\nL109:     * 【66†Share on WhatsApp - What are European elections? How are MEPs elected?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL110:     * Copy URL\nL111: \nL112:   * ## What is the European Parliament?\nL113: The European Parliament is the world’s only directly elected transnational assembly. The Members of the European Parliament represent the interests of EU citizens at the European level.\nL114: \nL115: How the European Parliament works\nL116: \nL117: Transcript - How the European Parliament works\nL118: \nL119: What is the European Parliament?\nL120: \nL121: It’s the only EU body directly elected by the people!\nL122: \nL123: Every 5 years, voters choose over 700 members - or MEPs - to represent nearly 450 million Europeans.\nL124: MEPs work on your behalf, debating, shaping and passing laws on issues central to our daily lives.\nL125: \nL126: Parliament defends freedom, equality and the rule of law across the EU and promotes democracy and human rights around the world.\nL127: \nL128: It elects the president of the European Commission and makes sure EU institutions are doing their job properly.\nL129: \nL130: MEPs decide how the EU’s money is spent and sign off on the EU budget.\nL131: \nL132: The European Parliament is your link to the decisions that matter!\nL133: \nL134: Democracy in action\nL135: European Parliament\nL136: \nL137: Share\nL138: \nL139:     * 【67†Share on Facebook - What is the European Parliament?†www.facebook.com】\nL140:     * 【68†Share on Twiter - What is the European Parliament?†twitter.com】\nL141:     * 【69†Share on LinkedIn - What is the European Parliament?†www.linkedin.com】\nL142:     * 【70†Share on WhatsApp - What is the European Parliament?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL143:     * Copy URL\nL144: \nL145:   * ## What powers does the European Parliament have?\nL146: Together with representatives of the governments of EU countries, MEPs 【71†shape and decide on new laws†www.europarl.europa.eu】 that influence all aspects of lives across the European Union, from supporting the economy and the fight against poverty to climate change and security.\nL147: \nL148: MEPs put important political, economic and social topics in the spotlight and uphold the values of the European Union: respect for human rights, freedom, democracy, equality and the rule of law.\nL149: Parliament approves the EU budget and scrutinises how the money is spent. It also elects the President of the European Commission, appoints its Commissioners and holds them to account.\nL150: \nL151: The role of Members of the European Parliament\nL152: \nL153: Transcript - The role of Members of the European Parliament\nL154: \nL155: What do MEPs do for you?\nL156: \nL157: They’re your link to decisions that shape the EU!\nL158: \nL159: You vote for them, then they vote for you in the European Parliament.\nL160: That’s where MEPs work to protect your interests, improving and passing the laws that affect your daily lives.\nL161: \nL162: MEPs are there to listen to people’s concerns and act on them, to consult with civil society and businesses, making sure everyone has a voice at EU level.\nL163: \nL164: And remember:\nL165: \nL166: MEPs represent you.\nL167: \nL168: You choose them by voting in the European elections, every five years.\nL169: \nL170: Democracy in action\nL171: \nL172: European Parliament\nL173: \nL174: Share\nL175:     * 【72†Share on Facebook - What powers does the European Parliament have?†www.facebook.com】\nL176:     * 【73†Share on Twiter - What powers does the European Parliament have?†twitter.com】\nL177:     * 【74†Share on LinkedIn - What powers does the European Parliament have?†www.linkedin.com】\nL178:     * 【75†Share on WhatsApp - What powers does the European Parliament have?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL179:     * Copy URL\nL180: \nL181:   * ## How often are European elections held?\nL182: \nL183: European elections are held every five years.\nL184: \nL185: Share\nL186:     * 【76†Share on Facebook - How often are European elections held?†www.facebook.com】\nL187:     * 【77†Share on Twiter - How often are European elections held?†twitter.com】\nL188:     * 【78†Share on LinkedIn - How often are European elections held?†www.linkedin.com】\nL189:     * 【79†Share on WhatsApp - How often are European elections held?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL190:     * Copy URL\nL191: \nL192:   * ## When are the next European elections?\nL193: \nL194: The next European elections will be held in 2029.\nL195: \nL196: Share\nL197:     * 【80†Share on Facebook - When are the next European elections?†www.facebook.com】\nL198:     * 【81†Share on Twiter - When are the next European elections?†twitter.com】\nL199:     * 【82†Share on LinkedIn - When are the next European elections?†www.linkedin.com】\nL200:     * 【83†Share on WhatsApp - When are the next European elections?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL201:     * Copy URL\nL202: \nL203:   * ## What European election rules are common in all EU countries?\nL204: It is up to each country to manage the election, but there are some common principles they must apply.\nL205: \nL206:     * Elections take place during a four-day period, from Thursday to Sunday.\nL207:     * The number of MEPs elected from a political party is proportional to the number of votes it receives.\nL208:     * EU citizens resident in another EU country can vote and stand for election there.\nL209:     * Each citizen can vote only once.\nL210: \nL211: Find out 【84†how the European elections are organised in your country】.\nL212: \nL213: Share\nL214:     * 【85†Share on Facebook - What European election rules are common in all EU countries?†www.facebook.com】\nL215:     * 【86†Share on Twiter - What European election rules are common in all EU countries?†twitter.com】\nL216:     * 【87†Share on LinkedIn - What European election rules are common in all EU countries?†www.linkedin.com】\nL217:     * 【88†Share on WhatsApp - What European election rules are common in all EU countries?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL218:     * Copy URL\nL219: \nL220:   * ## How many MEPs are elected?\nL221: A total of 720 MEPs were elected in June 2024, 15 more compared to the previous elections.\nL222: \nL223: As a general rule, the number of MEPs is decided before each election. The total cannot exceed 750 plus the president.\nL224: \nL225: Share\nL226:     * 【89†Share on Facebook - How many MEPs are elected?†www.facebook.com】\nL227:     * 【90†Share on Twiter - How many MEPs are elected?†twitter.com】\nL228:     * 【91†Share on LinkedIn - How many MEPs are elected?†www.linkedin.com】\nL229:     * 【92†Share on WhatsApp - How many MEPs are elected?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL230:     * Copy URL\nL231: \nL232:   * ## How many MEPs does each country elect?\nL233: \nL234: Here are the number of MEPs that were elected in each country in the 2024 European elections:\nL235:     * Germany: 96\nL236:     * France: 81\nL237:     * Italy: 76\nL238:     * Spain: 61\nL239:     * Poland: 53\nL240:     * Romania: 33\nL241:     * Netherlands: 31\nL242:     * Belgium: 22\nL243:     * Greece: 21\nL244:     * Czechia: 21\nL245:     * Sweden: 21\nL246:     * Portugal: 21\nL247:     * Hungary: 21\nL248:     * Austria: 20\nL249:     * Bulgaria: 17\nL250:     * Denmark: 15\nL251:     * Finland: 15\nL252:     * Slovakia: 15\nL253:     * Ireland: 14\nL254:     * Croatia: 12\nL255:     * Lithuania: 11\nL256:     * Slovenia: 9\nL257:     * Latvia: 9\nL258:     * Estonia: 7\nL259:     * Cyprus: 6\nL260:     * Luxembourg: 6\nL261:     * Malta: 6\nL262: The number of MEPs elected from each EU country is agreed before each election and is based on the principle of degressive proportionality, which means each MEP from a larger country represents more people than an MEP from a smaller country. The minimum number of MEPs from any country is six and the maximum number is 96.\nL263: \nL264: Share\nL265:     * 【93†Share on Facebook - How many MEPs does each country elect?†www.facebook.com】\nL266:     * 【94†Share on Twiter - How many MEPs does each country elect?†twitter.com】\nL267:     * 【95†Share on LinkedIn - How many MEPs does each country elect?†www.linkedin.com】\nL268:     * 【96†Share on WhatsApp - How many MEPs does each country elect?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL269:     * Copy URL\nL270: \nL271:   * ## Do we vote for national parties or European parties?\nL272: Elections are contested by national political parties but once MEPs are elected, most opt to become part of transnational political groups. Most national parties are affiliated to a European-wide political party.\nL273: \nL274: Share\nL275:     * 【97†Share on Facebook - Do we vote for national parties or European parties?†www.facebook.com】\nL276:     * 【98†Share on Twiter - Do we vote for national parties or European parties?†twitter.com】\nL277:     * 【99†Share on LinkedIn - Do we vote for national parties or European parties?†www.linkedin.com】\nL278:     * 【100†Share on WhatsApp - Do we vote for national parties or European parties?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL279:     * Copy URL\nL280: \nL281:   * ## What are the political groups in the European Parliament?\nL282: MEPs sit in political groups based on shared ideals. Each group has a minimum of 23 MEPs from at least a quarter of EU countries. There are 【101†eight groups in the current Parliament†www.europarl.europa.eu】.\nL283: \nL284: Share\nL285:     * 【102†Share on Facebook - What are the political groups in the European Parliament?†www.facebook.com】\nL286:     * 【103†Share on Twiter - What are the political groups in the European Parliament?†twitter.com】\nL287:     * 【104†Share on LinkedIn - What are the political groups in the European Parliament?†www.linkedin.com】\nL288:     * 【105†Share on WhatsApp - What are the political groups in the European Parliament?†api.whatsapp.com】\nL289:     * Copy URL\nL290: \nL291:   * ## Where can I find the results of past elections?\nL292: The election results are available on the 【3†election results website†results.elections.europa.eu】.\nL293: \nL294: Share\nL295: \n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nHow federal impeachment works | USAGov (https://www.usa.gov/impeachment)\nciteturn14view2 [wordlim: 200] Crawled: today; Content type: text/html; Source: open({\"ref_id\":\"https://www.usa.gov/impeachment\",\"lineno\":null}); Total lines: 105\nL0: 【0†GTM†www.googletagmanager.com】 【1†Skip to main content】\nL1: \nL2: 【2†Image: Close】\nL3: \nL4:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL5:   * 【4†The U.S. and its government】\nL6:   * 【5†Government benefits】\nL7:   * 【6†Immigration and U.S. citizenship】\nL8:   * 【7†Money and credit】\nL9:   * 【8†Travel】\nL10:   * 【9†Voting and elections】\nL11:   * 【10†Laws and legal issues】\nL12:     * 【11†Request copies of vital records and ID cards】\nL13:     * 【12†Find a lawyer and affordable legal aid】\nL14:     * 【13†Family legal issues】\nL15:     * 【14†Prisons and prisoners】\nL16:     * 【15†Crime reporting, victim support, and crime statistics】\nL17:     * 【16†Authenticate an official document】\nL18:     * 【17†State attorneys general】\nL19:     * 【18†Federal laws and regulations】\nL20:       * 【19†Research federal laws】\nL21:       * 【20†How laws are made】\nL22:       * 【21†Your government files through the Privacy Act】\nL23:       * 【22†Copyrighted government works】\nL24:       * current page\nL25: Impeachment process\nL26: \nL27:     * 【23†Federal, state, territory, county, and municipal courts】\nL28: # How federal impeachment works\nL29: \nL30: Impeachment is the process of bringing charges against a government official for wrongdoing. A trial may be held, and the official may be removed from office.\nL31: ## The impeachment process\nL32: \nL33: The Constitution gives Congress the power to impeach federal officials. An official can be impeached for treason, bribery, and “other high crimes and misdemeanors.”\nL34: \nL35:   1. The House of Representatives brings articles (charges) of impeachment against an official. 【24†Learn more about the House’s role in impeachment.†history.house.gov】\nL36: \nL37:   2. If the House adopts the articles by a simple majority vote, the official has been impeached.\nL38:   3. The Senate holds an impeachment trial. In the case of a president, the U.S. Supreme Court chief justice presides. Learn more about the 【25†Senate’s role in the impeachment process†www.senate.gov】.\nL39: \nL40:   4. If found guilty, the official is removed from office. They may never be able to hold elected office again.\nL41: \nL42:   5. If they are not found guilty, they may continue to serve in office.\nL43: 【26†Learn more about impeachment†www.congress.gov】, including its history and how the U.S. Constitution grants impeachment powers to Congress.\nL44: ## Past impeachments of federal officials\nL45: \nL46: The House has initiated impeachment proceedings more than 60 times. But there have been only 21 impeachments. This includes three presidents, one cabinet secretary, and one senator. Of those who were impeached, only eight officials were found guilty by the Senate and removed from office. All eight were federal judges.\nL47: \nL48: The presidents impeached by the House were:\nL49:   * 【27†Andrew Johnson†www.archives.gov】 in 1868\nL50:   * 【28†William Jefferson (Bill) Clinton†www.clintonlibrary.gov】 in 1998\nL51:   * 【29†Donald John Trump in 2019†www.congress.gov】 and 【30†2021†www.congress.gov】\nL52: \nL53: Presidents Johnson, Clinton, and Trump remained in office following acquittals by the Senate on all charges.\nL54: \nL55: Former President Richard Nixon was not impeached. He resigned after Congress started the impeachment process against him in 1974.\nL56: ## Impeachment of state and local officials\nL57: \nL58: A state legislature can impeach its governor and other state officials. Many local governments also have impeachment procedures. 【31†Find your state legislature’s website†www.congress.gov】 to learn more.\nL59: \nL60: \nL61: \nL62: LAST UPDATED: November 13, 2025\nL63: \nL64: SHARE THIS PAGE:\nL65: \nL66: ## Have a question?\nL67: \nL68: Ask a real person any government-related question for free. They will get you the answer or let you know where to find it.\nL69: \nL70: 【32†Call USAGov】\nL71: \nL72: 【33†Chat with USAGov】\nL73: \nL74: 【34†Top】\nL75: ### Government information\nL76: \nL77:   * 【3†All topics and services】\nL78:   * 【35†Directory of U.S. government agencies and departments】\nL79:   * 【36†Branches of government】\nL80: \nL81: ### About us\nL82: \nL83:   * 【37†About USAGov】\nL84:   * 【38†Report a website issue】\nL85:   * 【39†Website usage data】\nL86: \nL87: ### For federal agencies\nL88: \nL89:   * 【40†Partner with us】\nL90:   * 【41†Read our blog】\nL91: #### USAGov Contact Center\nL92: \nL93: Ask USA.gov a question at\nL94: \nL95: 【32†1-844-USAGOV1 (1-844-872-4681)】\nL96: \nL97: USAGov is the official guide to government information and services\nL98: \nL99: An official website of the 【42†U.S. General Services Administration†www.gsa.gov】\nL100: \nL101: This website complies with Executive Order 【43†14224†www.federalregister.gov】. English is the official language of the U.S. Government, and the English-language version of this website is the authoritative source for all federal information contained herein.\nL102:   * 【44†Accessibility policy】\nL103:   * 【45†Privacy and security policies】\nL104:   * 【46†FOIA requests†www.gsa.gov】\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nConfidence motion - UK Parliament (